MLX's CUDA backend has no fused attention past head dim 128: the vector
kernel takes 64/96/128 and cuDNN is capped at 128, so Gemma 4 (256 sliding,
512 global) and the Qwen3.5 family (256) run every attention call on the
unfused fallback, which materializes the score matrix. This patch:

- runs the vector kernel (1..3 query rows) at head dims 256 and 512, with
  vectorized per-lane row loads (one 16-byte load instead of eight scalar
  ones) and __launch_bounds__(1024) so the 512 variants fit the 1024-thread
  block's register budget;
- lets cuDNN take head dim 256 on sm_120 (verified with cuDNN 9.27), which
  serves prefill-sized blocks 2-3x faster than the fallback;
- keeps cuDNN off 2..15 query rows: its graph is cached per shape, so a
  speculative-verify block over a growing kv cache rebuilt it every step
  (milliseconds of encode per attention layer).

Upstream PR pending (the user submits it); drop this file and its step in
build-mlx-linux.sh once lib/mlx-src includes it.

